# <%= current_page.data.title %>

The Justice Data Platform uses a project-based system which will help keep your data assets secure, reproducible and scalable.
For the first version of the Justice Data Platform, a project helps you organise and control access to models from the service's AI gateway.

When you create a project, you can:

* add members to it (optional)
* create an API key
* set the business unit it’s associated with

You can only add members who have previously signed into the Justice Data Platform and have an `@justice.gov.uk` email address.

Once you have a working API key, you can use it to access AI models.

## Check which endpoint you need to use

The Justice Data Platform’s AI gateway has two endpoints:
* an internal endpoint for users connecting from MOJ services
* a standard endpoint for any users

You should use the internal endpoint if you're connecting from MOJ's Cloud Platform or Modernisation Platform. You can access
it from: `https://internal.ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk`.

Use the standard endpoint if you're connecting from any other environment. You can access it from: `https://ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk`.

## Create an API key for your project

After creating your project, follow the steps on the Justice Data Platform to create an API key. Make sure you save the API key in a secure place.

## Check your API key works

After you’ve created your API key, make a basic request to the Justice Data Platform’s AI gateway to check it works. You can do this by completing the steps below.

### Test your API key in your IDE

1. Follow OpenAI’s documentation to [install an official SDK](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/libraries?#install-an-official-sdk) for your language.

1. Set your API key as an environment variable using the following command:

    ```bash
    export AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY="<your-api-key>"
    ```

1. Send an API request in your IDE. You can use the Python example request below, replacing `<model-name>` with the model you want to use. You should replace the
standard endpoint with the internal endpoint if you are connecting from MOJ's Cloud Platform or Modernisation Platform.

    ```python
    import os
    from openai import OpenAI

    client = OpenAI(
        api_key=os.environ["AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY"],
        base_url="https://ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk/",
    )

    response = client.responses.create(
        model="<model-name>",
        input="Reply with: Your API key is working",
    )

    print(response.output_text)
    ```

1. Check the response. If successful, you’ll receive `Your API key is working` or a similar response.

### Test your API key with `curl`

If the request in your IDE is successful, you do not need to do this step. You can use it to help identify whether a problem is with your API key or the Justice Data Platform.

1. Set your API key as an environment variable using the following command:

    ```bash
    export AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY="<your-api-key>"
    ```

1. Send a request using `curl`, replacing placeholders in the example below with your API key and the model you want to use. You should
also replace the standard endpoint with the internal endpoint if you are connecting from MOJ's Cloud Platform or Modernisation Platform.

     ```bash
     curl https://ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk/responses \
       -H "Authorization: Bearer $AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY" \
       -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
       -d '{
         "model": "<model-name>",
         "input": "Reply with: Your API key is working"
       }'
    ```

1. Check the response. If successful, you’ll receive `Your API key is working` or a similar response.

### Error handling for API calls

If you encounter an error when making requests to the Justice Data Platform’s AI gateway, check the following:

* make sure your API key is correctly set as an environment variable
* check you are using the correct model name
* check the [status page](https://status.data-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/posts/dashboard) for any ongoing incidents

Raise an issue in [the #ask-data-platform Slack channel](https://moj.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0B949G0J2X) if the problem continues, including your Project ID and Key ID (not your API key). You can find these by going to *Projects* in the Justice Data Platform and selecting your project.
